<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Rouge-1 and Rouge-L scores for different SFT configurations of Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct across five tasks. Gray-highlighted cells indicate the scores when the training task matches the test task, demonstrating task-specific effectiveness.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5 Results ‣ Brittleness and Promise: Knowledge Graph–Based Reward Modeling for Diagnostic Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the highlighted cells correspond to models that were both finetuned and evaluated on the same setting, where strong performance relative to other models would generally be expected. Interestingly, for the <span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">P@2</span> and <span id="S5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PN@10</span> settings, the finetuned models perform worse than their non-SFT counterparts. Moreover, the <span id="S5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">NHP</span> and <span id="S5.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PC</span> settings appear to be the most challenging, exhibiting the lowest overall performance across models.</p>
</div>
